course: dictionary
lesson: 4682
title: Homeland and home
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A homeland is the country where you or your family were born or come from. It can also be the land a people or nation see as their own. When you meet this word, look at the words around it for clues.
explain: «Donkeys bray to call other donkeys, and the sound can carry about 3 kilometres across their desert homeland.» The clues are desert and across: a homeland is a big stretch of land where donkeys live and belong. Swap in your meaning: "across their country where they belong" still makes sense.
explain: Homeland often appears in history and news. Refugees are people who have had to leave their homeland, often because it is not safe. Even when they are far away, the word tells you that place still matters to them.
fact: A homeland is the country where you or your family were born or come from.
fact: «Homeland» can also mean the land a people or nation see as their own.
fact: Home is where you live now; a homeland is where you or your family come from.
fact: «Donkeys bray to call other donkeys, and the sound can carry about 3 kilometres across their desert homeland.»
fact: Homeland is a noun: one homeland, two homelands.
exercise: «Amira's grandmother was born in a small village in Pakistan, and she still calls Pakistan her homeland.» What does homeland mean here? | *The country where her family were born | A kind of school | A shop that sells food | Any place she visits on holiday
exercise: «Refugees far from their homeland often miss the smell of home cooking.» Find and copy one word that means the country a person comes from. | smell | *homeland | village | cooking
exercise: What is a homeland? | *The country where you or your family were born or come from | A holiday place you have never seen | A school trip | A type of bird
test: «The refugees missed their homeland, which was far across the sea.» What are the refugees missing? | Their school | *The country they came from | A holiday they have not had | Their new bicycle
test: Jayden says homeland is just another word for home. What is the best reply? | Yes, they always mean exactly the same thing | *Not quite: home can be where you live now, but a homeland is where you or your family come from | Yes, but only for donkeys | No: a homeland is only a farm
exam: Which sentence uses homeland correctly? | *The family returned to their homeland after many years abroad | The homeland was a cake | Homeland is a loud noise made by donkeys | I ate my homeland for lunch
